{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run1:labor:inside","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:cf30f8f720a65d30d3d9f6bf3becf4938a097b2c21de24be02d70b8534dfc17e","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.28},"questionHash":"sha256:cfe468fa9439bedeee3804a35583667745f13831322e2c91a89c02a1b211413b","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq5","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Conjunct A: MOVE averaging >=105 over a full 10 years. Mechanism for structurally higher rate vol: (i) the inflation process becomes two-sided rather than one-sided - care/construction bottleneck inflation at +0.28pp/yr pushing up against China/AI goods deflation pushing down, so each data print is informative about which force dominates, which is exactly what raises implied rate vol; (ii) supply-driven duration risk from 2A debt dynamics with a price-insensitive marginal buyer absent; (iii) levered basis-trade intermediation converts flow shocks into yield gaps. Against: vol is multiplicative and mean-reverts hard in quiet years, and a 10-year AVERAGE of 105 requires roughly half the window at stress levels. Marginal 0.42. Conjunct B: at least one episode with MSCI World -25% peak-to-trough where G7 10y nominals fail to fall >=50bp (or rise >=25bp) inside the drawdown window. Sub-step 1: P(at least one -25% drawdown in 10 years) = 0.72 on mechanism - 2B AI-Q4 at 66% already posits an impairment/revenue-coverage failure, index concentration in AI-levered names is high, and the $1.4T commitment stack rests on ~$300B equity and ~$650B debt, so the equity leg is nearly implied by the locked priors. Sub-step 2: P(the no-rally type | a -25% drawdown occurs) = 0.55. A pure capex-bust drawdown is disinflationary and bonds rally hard (that is the D regime and it FAILS the condition); the no-rally version requires the drawdown to be driven by, or to collide with, the fiscal/term-premium channel - a Debt-at-Risk repricing, a care/pension fiscal claim shock, or an inflation-bottleneck print. Given my CQ4 term-premium mechanism and the fact that over 10 years there are likely 2-3 drawdown episodes of differing type, 0.55 for at least one being no-rally. Conjunct B = 0.72 x 0.55 = 0.40. CORRELATION: strongly positive, rho ~ +0.55, because the same fiscal/term-premium mechanism generates both high rate vol and the bond-fails-to-rally signature; P(A|B) ~ 0.70. Joint = 0.40 x 0.70 = 0.28. CQ5 = 28.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:51.154Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"US NCLEX-RN first-time candidate volume plus OECD-wide nursing-programme completions: do annual completions in the US, Germany, Japan, Korea and the UK rise by more than 25% above their 2025 level, with the binding constraint (clinical placement slots and nursing faculty posts) publicly reported as eased rather than worsened? Publicly observable via NCSBN annual NCLEX statistics, OECD Health Statistics 'nursing graduates', and national regulator registers.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2031\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the single cleanest falsifier of my weakest-link claim. If completions are up >25% AND placement/faculty capacity is reported as eased, my e_supply of 0.60 and my faculty/preceptor bottleneck are both wrong. CQ3 P50 moves up ~+0.25pp to ~2.05%, CQ6-A rises from 12 to ~20 and C falls from 23 to ~17, CQ1's unemployment conjunct strengthens while its wage conjunct weakens (more supply, less wage pressure), leaving CQ1 roughly flat near 16, and CQ2's OECD leg drops ~4pp because the Baumol pass-through shrinks.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 care-sector worker admissions by credential-recognition route: do combined annual entries of foreign-trained nurses, aides and personal-care workers exceed 1.2M/yr in any single year, and does mean time-to-licensure for an India- or Philippines-trained nurse fall below 12 months in at least three of the US, Germany, Japan, Canada and the UK? Observable via OECD International Migration Outlook, national regulator throughput statistics, and Philippine POEA/DMW deployment data.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies my administrative-throughput cap of 400-700k/yr and my entries-to-wage elasticity of ~0.1. The care constraint then clears substantially on migration rather than on wages or rationing, so: CQ1's wage conjunct falls from 0.25 to ~0.12 (migrant entry dilutes the F+Q median hard) and CQ1 drops from 16 to ~9; CQ2's OECD leg falls ~5pp and CQ2 to ~32; CQ3 P50 up ~+0.15pp; and hypothesis plank (b) rises from 13 toward ~25 on the remittance leg while falling further on the 'blocks' wording.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Philippine IT-BPM headcount and revenue (IBPAP annual roadmap data) and Indian IT-BPM export revenue (NASSCOM): does Philippine BPO headcount fall below 1.3M while sector revenue falls more than 15% below its 2026 level in nominal USD, AND do Philippine remittances fail to exceed $45bn/yr?\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2033\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Confirms the virtual leg's downside while falsifying my physical-leg offset, which is the specific netting my score of 13 on plank (b) rests on. Plank (b) would move from 13 to ~45 (the 'severely' word becomes defensible even though 'blocks' stays wrong). It would also mean the OECD care gap is NOT being filled by migration, so CQ1's wage conjunct rises from 0.25 to ~0.34 and CQ1 from 16 to ~21, CQ2's OECD leg rises ~4pp, and CQ6-C rises from 23 to ~28 at B's expense.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Divergence check on the actual CQ1 wage measure: OECD/national real median hourly earnings by ISIC sector. Does the cumulative 2025-to-year-end real median hourly gap between (F+Q) and (J+K+M) exceed +5.0pp, and is the DECOMPOSITION published showing whether within-job wage growth or composition dominates?\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A gap above +5.0pp by 2032 implies my composition wedge of -7 to -9pp is overstated and the path to +10pp by 2036 is open: CQ1's wage conjunct rises from 0.25 to ~0.45 and CQ1 from 16 to ~27. A gap below +2.0pp by 2032 confirms the composition offset and takes CQ1 down to ~9. Either way this is the signpost that most directly adjudicates my own CQ1.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"OECD-38 long-duration (12+ month) unemployment share among workers aged 45-59 with tertiary education in ISIC J, K and M, versus the same cohort's 2025 level, plus the share of their 3-year occupational destinations falling in ISIC F or Q (observable via national longitudinal labour-force microdata releases, e.g. US CPS/ACS, EU-LFS, and OECD Employment Outlook special chapters).\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"A measured 3-year F/Q destination share above 15% falsifies my e_cross = 0.07 outright and is the most damaging single observation to this entire effort: plank (a) would rise from 4 to ~30, CQ3 P50 up ~+0.2pp, CQ6-B falls toward ~40 as A and the benign part of B's residual gain, and the AI-Q5 coupling tension I report below reverses sign. A destination share below 5% with rising long-duration unemployment confirms the mechanism and pushes CQ6-B to ~52 and CQ5 up ~3pp through the demand-shortfall channel.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note":"run1 wave 1 inside seat, effort 'labor'."}}